# cella.policy — the crossings this task is granted.
# <release|refuse> <incoming|outgoing> <destination> (key=value)*
# keys: keep_open=<90s|5m|24h> skip_freeze=true reason="..."
# '*' matches any ip or any port. Everything not granted is refused.
#
# Every grant is exact, and every flow is a round trip at this border:
# the request parks outgoing and the reply parks in the ingress lane,
# so a granted destination needs its reply twin. keep_open + skip_freeze
# on the outgoing leg keep the machine live through the handshake --
# what keeps a real peer's TLS inside its patience -- instead of a
# freeze per crossing. Everything else is refused, on the record.
release outgoing arp (keep_open=24h) (skip_freeze=true)
release incoming arp (keep_open=24h)
release outgoing 1.1.1.1:443/tcp (keep_open=5m) (skip_freeze=true)
release incoming 1.1.1.1:443/tcp (keep_open=5m)
# The verifier probes an ungranted destination and asserts it stays
# refused. A negative probe MUST ride a standing refusal: skip_freeze on
# the refuse line answers every retransmit live, so the guest pays one
# freeze and no more, instead of a full deep re-warm per SYN retransmit
# (cella docs MEMBRANE-MEMORY.md -- "a policy that probes negatives MUST
# use the standing refusal"). A bare refusal is legal but deliberately
# costly; a probed negative is exactly where the window earns its keep.
refuse outgoing 8.8.8.8:443/tcp (keep_open=24h) (skip_freeze=true) (reason="ungranted, probed by the verifier")
